      "dom_id": "question-140438",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)={5}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2$,  find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Applying the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x}+2{e}^{x}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({5}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2) \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & ={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & =({5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2{e}^{x}){|}_{x=1} \\\\  & ={5}^{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2\\cdot {e}^{1} \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡5+2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
